  journal = {Wikipedia},
  urldate = {2025-11-12},
  abstract = {In mathematics and physics, Laplace's equation is a second-order partial differential equation named after Pierre-Simon Laplace, who first studied its properties in 1786. This is often written as                                   ∇                        2                                      f         =         0                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle \textbackslash nabla \textasciicircum\textbraceleft 2\textbraceright\textbackslash!f=0\textbraceright{}     or                         {$\Delta$}         f         =         0         ,                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle \textbackslash Delta f=0,\textbraceright{}    where                         {$\Delta$}         =         ∇         {$\cdot$}         ∇         =                    ∇                        2                                     \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle \textbackslash Delta =\textbackslash nabla \textbackslash cdot \textbackslash nabla =\textbackslash nabla \textasciicircum\textbraceleft 2\textbraceright\textbraceright{}     is the Laplace operator,                         ∇         {$\cdot$}                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle \textbackslash nabla \textbackslash cdot \textbraceright{}     is the divergence operator (also symbolized "div"),                         ∇                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle \textbackslash nabla \textbraceright{}     is the gradient operator (also symbolized "grad"), and                         f         (         x         ,         y         ,         z         )                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle f(x,y,z)\textbraceright{}     is a twice-differentiable real-valued function. The Laplace operator therefore maps a scalar function to another scalar function. If the right-hand side is specified as a given function,                         h         (         x         ,         y         ,         z         )                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle h(x,y,z)\textbraceright{}    , we have                        {$\Delta$}         f         =         h                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle \textbackslash Delta f=h\textbraceright{}    This is called Poisson's equation, a generalization of Laplace's equation. Laplace's equation and Poisson's equation are the simplest examples of elliptic partial differential equations. Laplace's equation is also a special case of the Helmholtz equation. The general theory of solutions to Laplace's equation is known as potential theory. The twice continuously differentiable solutions of Laplace's equation are the harmonic functions, which are important in multiple branches of physics, notably electrostatics, gravitation, and fluid dynamics. In the study of heat conduction, the Laplace equation is the steady-state heat equation. In general, Laplace's equation describes situations of equilibrium, or those that do not depend explicitly on time.},
  copyright = {Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike License},
  langid = {english},
  annotation = {Page Version ID: 1303458496\\
Read\_Status: To Read\\
