  journal = {Wikipedia},
  urldate = {2025-11-12},
  abstract = {In linear algebra and functional analysis, a projection is a linear transformation                         P                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle P\textbraceright{}     from a vector space to itself (an endomorphism) such that                         P         {$\circ$}         P         =         P                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle P\textbackslash circ P=P\textbraceright{}    . That is, whenever                         P                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle P\textbraceright{}     is applied twice to any vector, it gives the same result as if it were applied once (i.e.                         P                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle P\textbraceright{}     is idempotent). It leaves its image unchanged. This definition of "projection" formalizes and generalizes the idea of graphical projection.  One can also consider the effect of a projection on a geometrical object by examining the effect of the projection on points in the object.},
