  abstract = {In mathematical analysis, semicontinuity (or semi-continuity) is a property of extended real-valued functions that is weaker than continuity. An extended real-valued function                         f                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle f\textbraceright{}     is upper (respectively, lower) semicontinuous at a point                                    x                        0                                     \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle x\_\textbraceleft 0\textbraceright\textbraceright{}     if, roughly speaking, the function values for arguments near                                    x                        0                                     \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle x\_\textbraceleft 0\textbraceright\textbraceright{}     are not much higher (respectively, lower) than                         f                    (                        x                            0                                   )                  .                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle f\textbackslash left(x\_\textbraceleft 0\textbraceright\textbackslash right).\textbraceright{}     Briefly, a function on a domain                         X                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle X\textbraceright{}     is lower semi-continuous if its epigraph                         \textbraceleft{}         (         x         ,         t         )         {$\in$}         X         \texttimes{}                    R                  :         t         {$\geq$}         f         (         x         )         \textbraceright{}                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle \textbackslash\textbraceleft (x,t)\textbackslash in X\textbackslash times \textbackslash mathbb \textbraceleft R\textbraceright{} :t\textbackslash geq f(x)\textbackslash\textbraceright\textbraceright{}     is closed in                         X         \texttimes{}                    R                          \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle X\textbackslash times \textbackslash mathbb \textbraceleft R\textbraceright{} \textbraceright{}    , and upper semi-continuous if                         -         f                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle -f\textbraceright{}     is lower semi-continuous. A function is continuous if and only if it is both upper  and lower semicontinuous. If we take a continuous function and increase its value at a certain point                                    x                        0                                     \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle x\_\textbraceleft 0\textbraceright\textbraceright{}     to                         f                    (                        x                            0                                   )                  +         c                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle f\textbackslash left(x\_\textbraceleft 0\textbraceright\textbackslash right)+c\textbraceright{}     for some                         c         {$>$}         0                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle c{$>$}0\textbraceright{}    , then the result is upper semicontinuous; if we decrease its value to                         f                    (                        x                            0                                   )                  -         c                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle f\textbackslash left(x\_\textbraceleft 0\textbraceright\textbackslash right)-c\textbraceright{}     then the result is lower semicontinuous. The notion of upper and lower semicontinuous function was first introduced and studied by Ren\'e Baire in his thesis in 1899.},
